server, win32u, winewayland: a window takes mouse input only where its shape is, as on Windows.

Filmora 16's first-run walkthrough could not be followed (lab, 2026-11-03, Wayland driver): every click and drag went to the
walkthrough itself and nothing under it could be used. The walkthrough is "FMaskWindow", a WS_EX_LAYERED per-pixel-alpha
popup over the whole main window, dimmed except for fully transparent holes over the control the user is asked to use
("Import Material", "Drag to edit", "Drag to add effect", "Play and preview", "Export video"). Windows hit-tests a layered
window by its shape: its color-keyed pixels and the fully transparent pixels of a per-pixel-alpha layered window let the
mouse through ("Layered Windows", Microsoft Learn), as do the pixels outside a window region. Wine ignored the pixels in
both places a click is routed:

- the compositor: the Wayland input region was the whole surface (empty only for WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TRANSPARENT), so the
  compositor gave every click on the transparent holes to the walkthrough's surface;
- the server: is_point_in_window skipped only WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TRANSPARENT windows, so WindowFromPoint returned the
  walkthrough at the holes, and OLE drag and drop, which finds its drop target with WindowFromPoint, refused every drop
  onto the timeline under it.

win32u already computes the shape before every flush (set_surface_shape: window region, color key, all-zero pixels; the
last is Neutron's rule for layered surfaces that mix GDI text into per-pixel alpha). Now:

- winewayland turns the surface shape into rects (rows banded) and sets them as the wl_surface input region, applied with
  the buffer at commit, in surface-local coordinates (scale and crop origin, rounded outward), re-applied when the shape,
  scale or crop origin changes and on every new wl_surface; WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TRANSPARENT stays an empty region. A
  flush that bails before the commit keeps the shape pending (win32u reports a change once).
- win32u reports a layered window's shape to the server when it changes (new request set_window_hit_region, appended;
  protocol 950), from the surface in the window's own dpi (a DPI-scaled window's wrapper, not its device-pixel target);
  is_point_in_window then skips a layered window's pixels outside it.

More than 4096 rects: the window takes input everywhere, as before. Same-process and cross-process WindowFromPoint both
follow the server. Not covered: a layered window whose only transparency is SetLayeredWindowAttributes constant alpha 0
(no shape is computed for it). Kill switch NEUTRON_SHAPE_HITTEST=0 (both halves).

Lab, Filmora 16.0.12 on 175 + this (PgI-filmora): the whole walkthrough, Import -> Open -> drag clip to the timeline ->
drag RGB Stroke onto it -> Play -> Export (file written), and the walkthrough's own close button still takes clicks. With
NEUTRON_SHAPE_HITTEST=0 on the same build the Import click is eaten, 2 of 2; with the input region alone (no server part)
clicks pass but the drop onto the timeline is refused. Photoshop 2026: New Document typed name + document created, the
"Generate Image" coach mark's "Got it" dismisses it. Animate 2024: New Document (CEP) as before.


